    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "body": "### Problem\n\nIn follow mode, `fa` tries to jump each cursor to the next instance `a`. And if the main cursor has an `a` after it, then it works, and all cursors with an `a` after them jump. But if the primary cursor _doesn't_ have an `a` after it, then the motion doesn't cascade, and the other cursors don't attempt to jump.\n\nThis regressed in d24cafc06a78f6357674c2429706303538be48da (bisected).\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n```sh\nnvim --clean\n:= vim.api.nvim_buf_set_lines(0, 0, -1, false, { \" a\", \"  b\", \"   a\", \"    b\" })\nvGQ\nfb\n# observe that two cursors jump - second one and last one\n0\nfa\n# observe that no cursors jump, even though the first and third one should\n```\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nThe motion would cascade, even if it didn't \"succeed\" on the primary cursor - since it may succeed on others. I think `f` shouldn't \"beep\", or at least its beep should be ignored. The same of course goes for `F` / `t` / `T`.\n\n### System info\n\n- Nvim version (nvim -v): nightly commit d24cafc06a78f6357674c2429706303538be48da\n- Vim (not Nvim) behaves the same: no, no multicursor\n- Operating system/version: NixOS unstable\n- Terminal name/version: Kitty 0.48.2\n- $TERM environment variable: xterm-kitty\n- Installation: nixpkgs",
    "closed_at": null,
    "closed_by": null,
    "comment_data": [
        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "idk, it seems potentially confusing for the other cursors to do things the primary cursor did not do.\n\nOTOH, https://github.com/neovim/neovim/commit/d24cafc06a78f6357674c2429706303538be48da is mostly to avoid spurious \"dedupe\", so maybe it's fine to \"follow\" a failed *horizontal* motion.\n\nThis also affects `x` if primary is on an empty line.\n\n> I think `f` shouldn't \"beep\", or at least its beep should be ignored. The same of course goes for `F` / `t` / `T`.\n\nHmm...",
